 \begin{abstract}
-An agent team can complete every assigned subtask yet miss what makes the final response good. Knowing the quality requirements is not the same as organizing to satisfy them: comparability, consistency, and evidence coverage can depend on how separate contributions interact. We introduce \method, which uses predicted task rubrics as a shared interface for team construction and cross-task improvement. Before execution, a global analyzer anticipates requirements, candidate agents negotiate responsibilities and information needs, and a coordinator compiles the reconciled plan into a task-specific JIT harness. After submission, independent criterion-level feedback is aligned with the frozen predictions and examined against distributed execution evidence. The same requirement links support two decisions: how to organize the current task, and what to change for future tasks. Proposed changes to rubric, organization, or execution experience are retained only after paired validation that rebuilds teams on separate tasks. Model parameters and the evaluator remain fixed. We specify controlled tests that hold answer guidance constant while varying rubric access during organization, and that evaluate updates beyond the failures that produced them. This separates the hypothesized value of rubric-mediated coordination from longer prompts, extra deliberation, and task-specific answer repair.
+Open-ended generation requires deciding not only what to write, but how agents should collaborate to produce it. When quality requirements are only partially specified, the central challenge is to anticipate what good work demands and turn those expectations into a suitable writing process. We introduce \method, an experience-driven framework that synthesizes an executable, task-specific multi-agent writing workflow on demand. Predicted rubrics serve both as a specification for workflow construction and as a reference for improving future construction. Before execution, global--local planning refines the requirements and their importance, negotiates agent responsibilities and information dependencies, and instantiates the resulting design as roles, interactions, and verification stages. After submission, independent criterion-level feedback is aligned with the frozen predictions and examined against global coordination records and agent-local trajectories. This supports targeted revisions to requirement prediction, team organization, or execution, rather than an undifferentiated instruction to improve the answer. Revisions enter reusable experience only after paired validation in which incumbent and candidate states independently construct and execute workflows on separate tasks. Model parameters and the evaluator remain fixed, while accepted experience changes how subsequent teams and workflows are generated. \method\ thus couples just-in-time task adaptation with cross-task evolution: it retains knowledge for constructing the next writing process, not a single process that every task must reuse.
 \end{abstract}
 
