 \section{Conclusion}
-
-\method\ treats task rubrics as more than criteria for an answer: they connect what a team anticipates, how it divides work, and what its evidence supports changing afterward. Global--local negotiation constructs this connection before execution; global--local diagnosis revisits it after independent feedback; validation determines which lessons may influence another task. The persistent object is reusable experience for constructing teams, while the teams themselves remain task-adaptive. The decisive empirical question is whether this connection improves coordination and transfer beyond identical rubric guidance and matched computation. The proposed controls expose that distinction without equating a completed subtask, a recorded commitment, or an accepted update with better final work.
+\label{sec:conclusion}
+
+\method\ couples just-in-time composition of multi-agent writing workflows with cross-task evolution of the experience used to construct them. Predicted quality requirements guide global--local team design and executable workflow synthesis; later criterion feedback is traced through the frozen expectations, responsibilities, and local evidence to propose targeted revisions. Paired validation determines which lessons may inform the next request. The persistent object is the synthesis experience, not one team or writing process. The decisive tests ask whether quality-conditioned construction adds value beyond identical answer guidance, and whether validated experience transfers to independently generated workflows on new tasks. The framework makes these questions testable without treating code generation, a recorded commitment, or a retained lesson as evidence of improved task quality.
